Why docker containers are not individually used in production?
In production, individual Docker containers are rarely used because they lack automation and resiliency. If a single container crashes, 
it stays down unless a human manually restarts it.

The Problem with Single Containers
No Self-Healing: If the process fails, the container stops and your app goes offline.
Manual Scaling: You can't easily add more "clones" to handle high traffic.
Zero Downtime: Updating a single container requires stopping it, causing a service gap.
Single Point of Failure: If the host machine dies, everything on it dies.

Why Orchestrators (Kubernetes/Swarm) are Used

Production environments use "orchestrators" to manage groups of containers automatically:

High Availability: They automatically restart failed containers on healthy servers.
Load Balancing: They distribute incoming traffic across multiple container instances.
Rolling Updates: They replace old containers with new ones one-by-one so the site never goes down.
Declarative State: You tell the system "I want 5 copies running," and the system makes it happen.

The Exception
You might run a single container in production only if:
It's a tiny, non-critical internal tool.
You are using a "Serverless" platform (like AWS Fargate or Google Cloud Run) where the provider acts as the orchestrator for you.

